why extra_sram is not being folded into Memory:
- Map::extra_sram/set_extra_sram no longer exist; 39df6b2 replaced them with
  save_sram/load_sram(&Memory, path) when BandaiFCG went to page tables. So
  the trait method this item was about is already gone.
- Folding the remaining case (Namco163's 128-byte sound RAM) into Src::ExRam
  means Audio::clock reads it, and that is reached from Map::clock ->
  Clock for Mapper -> Bus::cpu_clock, every CPU cycle. Map::clock would have
  to take &mut Memory for all 10 clocked boards so that exactly one could use
  it, Mapper could no longer impl Clock, and Bus::cpu_clock - the function
  the MapperOps work showed is right at LLVM's inlining threshold - grows a
  borrow split and an argument.
- It would not remove save_sram/load_sram from the trait anyway: BandaiFCG's
  battery covers EEPROM state machines, which are not memory and never will
  be in Memory.
- So: hot-path risk + trait unchanged, to delete ~20 lines. Revisit only if
  save_sram/load_sram need to go for another reason.

follow-ups found during phase 4:
- Regional for Mapper is dead. Nothing calls mapper.region()/set_region() -
  Ppu::set_region sets its own timing and mask but never forwards to the
  mapper. Exrom::set_region (-> dmc.set_region) is therefore never invoked,
  so MMC5 expansion audio does not track PAL/Dendy. Either wire it up (an
  emulation-behaviour change, needs snapshot review) or delete the dead path.
- Box SunsoftFme7? Mapper is 72 bytes and FME7 is what sets that; boxing it
  gives 56. Looks mildly favourable on non-FME7 ROMs but there is no mapper
  069 ROM in the corpus to measure the cost side. See benches/README.md.

